body:
> One solution would be for `disconnect` to look up the entry in `signal_map` itself, and check whether it is a persistent entry. Only if `_disconnect` returns true, AND it was a persistent entry, the change signal should be fired.
 
This requires `Node::disconnect` to access the private member `signal_data` from `Object`, I'd need to friend class it or add another function to `Object` to do so. I'm assuming that's not desired. `Object::_disconnect` was only `protected`, so on the original suggestion while I was squeamish about using something with an underscore prefix outside the class itself, friend classing wasn't needed.

> The other solution would be to return `Pair<bool, int>` of `{ was_removed, flags }` from `_disconnect`, where `flags` is the `flags` of the removed `SignalData` (if removed).

I guessed that this would add extra instructions to disconnects in non-editor builds, which I was trying to avoid, since adding instructions to non-editor builds to fix an editor only problem felt bad. The return from `_disconnect` is actually used in non-editor code, so I didn't trust the compiler to be smart enough to realize at link time that it could make a better version with no return to use for some cases, or to inline the whole thing since it's so large. That said, boy howdy does link time optimization take a long time on my builds, and I'm not nearly as familiar with its limits, so hey, maybe it's taking all that time to be super smart.

To check, I tried inspecting the assembly after compiling with `optimize=speed_trace production=yes` (and disabling the relevant `TOOLS_ENABLED` sections). For some reason, my usual disassembler crashed my entire operating system twice trying to open godot, and I needed to go to work, so I just banged out a quick benchmark:

```
static void old_bench() {
		const int BATCH_SIZE = 100;
		const int TRIALS = 1000000;
		int time_taken = 0;
		Control control[BATCH_SIZE];
		Callable callable(&control[0], "get_end");
		for (int j = 0; j < TRIALS; j++) {
			for (int i = 0; i < BATCH_SIZE; i++) {
				control[i].connect("item_rect_changed", callable);
			}
			int start_time = OS::get_singleton()->get_ticks_usec();
			for (int i = 0; i < BATCH_SIZE; i++) {
				control[i].Node::old_disconnect("item_rect_changed", callable);
			}
			time_taken += OS::get_singleton()->get_ticks_usec() - start_time;
		}
		print_line("Time taken (old disconnect): ", time_taken);
}
```

alongside a version with the `Pair<bool, int>` return, left them running alternating back and forth between the two in the same executable while I was at work, and checked back in over lunch.

It got restarted partway through for unrelated reasons.
|  |  Batch 1 (old) | Batch 1 (Pair) | Batch 2 (old) | Batch 2 (Pair) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Total disconnects: | 8.9B | 8.9B | 17.4B | 17.4B |
| Total time (min): | 28.9 | 29.9 | 56.8 | 58.9 |
| Time per 1k (ms) | 0.195 | 0.201 | 0.196 | 0.203 |

That's about a 3% slowdown. I also collected some profiler data before I ran this, and it showed a 3.4% slowdown in time spent in `_disconnect`, as well, despite this benchmark only testing a code path that doesn't actually use the return value of `_disconnect` at all.

The `Pair` version is almost certainly faster when used with `get_persistent_signal_connection_count` specifically, but in my opinion `get_persistent_signal_connection_count` is the lowest possible priority user of `_disconnect`, and it doesn't show up on editor flamegraphs even with the extra dictionary lookups the current PR has, so I don't think it's worth it.

I can do either of the proposed solutions if requested, but I'm not a particular fan of either of them for the reasons above.
